      <h1>How Much Does a Trademark Cost? 2026 USPTO Fee Breakdown</h1>
      <div class="entry-content">
<p><em>Reviewed by Avinash Mittal, Sulfab Law Office.</em></p>
<p>A trademark application filed with the USPTO costs $350 per class in government fees. Most small businesses need one class, so plan on $350 to start. Add attorney fees, and the realistic total to reach registration commonly runs from about $1,500 to $3,500. Fixing a rejected application costs more.</p>
<p>Type "how much does a trademark cost" into Google and most pages hand you that $350 figure and stop. The number is accurate. It is also the smallest line on the bill.</p>
<p>The rest comes from your classes, how carefully your goods are described, and what happens after you file. Here is the whole picture, including the parts that surprise people two months in.</p>
<h2 id="what-the-uspto-actually-charges">What the USPTO actually charges</h2>
<p>Every government fee comes from the USPTO's published fee schedule, so you can check any figure yourself before you file (<a href="https://www.uspto.gov/trademarks/trademark-fee-information">USPTO</a>). These are the ones that matter for a standard application.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Cost line</th><th>Typical amount</th><th>When it applies</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Base application filing fee</td><td>$350 per class</td><td>Every new Section 1 or Section 44 application</td></tr><tr><td>Additional class</td><td>$350 each</td><td>Each extra category of goods or services</td></tr><tr><td>Statement of Use (intent-to-use filings)</td><td>$150 per class</td><td>When you prove use after a Section 1(b) application</td></tr><tr><td>Free-form text box</td><td>$200 per class</td><td>When you write your own description instead of using the ID Manual</td></tr><tr><td>Insufficient information fee</td><td>$100 per class</td><td>When the description is too vague to examine</td></tr><tr><td>Petition to revive</td><td>$250</td><td>When an abandoned application is restored</td></tr><tr><td>Section 8 declaration of use</td><td>$325 per class</td><td>Between the fifth and sixth year after registration</td></tr><tr><td>Section 15 incontestability</td><td>$250 per class</td><td>Optional, after five years of continuous use</td></tr><tr><td>Ten-year renewal with Section 8 declaration</td><td>$650 per class</td><td>At each ten-year mark, as one combined filing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>The short version:</strong> one class, already in use, described from the ID Manual, $350 in government fees. Add $150 per class if you file before launch, $200 per class if you write your own description, and attorney time on top.</p>
<p>Note two things. The fee is per class, and it is not refundable. The $100 and $200 lines depend on how you describe your goods, not on what the examiner decides about your mark.</p>
<p>One more line matters if you file before you launch. An intent-to-use application under Section 1(b) pays the same $350 to file, then $150 per class when you submit the Statement of Use to prove you are actually selling. A six-month extension to file that statement costs $125 per class. Those are the costs a pre-launch founder most often misses.</p>
<h2 id="why-classes-multiply-the-cost">Why classes multiply the cost</h2>
<p>The USPTO sorts goods and services into classes. Each class is a distinct area of commerce. Clothing sits in one. Restaurant services sit in another. Software sits in a third.</p>
<p>Sell software and run a coffee shop under the same name and you are filing in two classes. That is $700 in government fees, not $350. The paperwork is not much longer. The bill is.</p>
<p>That is why two businesses can be quoted very different totals for the same "one trademark." A bakery in one class has little in common with a manufacturer filing across four.</p>
<p>Two rules of thumb:</p>
<ol><li>File in the classes where you sell now, or will sell soon under a documented plan.</li><li>Add classes as the business grows rather than guessing at everything on day one.</li></ol>
<p>Filing classes you never use is money spent on nothing. Filing too few leaves part of the business exposed. If you are unsure which classes your business actually touches, <a href="/intellectual-property-lawyer-san-francisco/">an intellectual property lawyer in San Francisco</a> can map them before you file.</p>
<p>The name and the logo also need separate applications, at $350 per class each, which is why many small businesses file the name first. And a trademark covers brand identifiers, not the content of your work. If you are unsure which protection fits, <a href="/blog/trademark-vs-copyright-whats-the-difference/">trademark versus copyright</a> explains the split.</p>
<h2 id="how-much-does-a-trademark-cost-once-you-add-attorney-fees">How much does a trademark cost once you add attorney fees?</h2>
<p>Attorney fees are not set by any agency, so treat every range here as a guide. The number moves with the description, your classes, and how the firm bills.</p>
<p>Flat fees are common because the scope is predictable. A flat fee for a straightforward application commonly covers:</p>
<ul><li>A clearance search, or a review of the search you ran yourself</li><li>An opinion on whether the mark is likely to register</li><li>Drafting the goods and services description from the USPTO's ID Manual</li><li>Filing the application through the USPTO's online system (<a href="https://www.uspto.gov/trademarks/apply">USPTO</a>)</li><li>Reporting the registration when it issues</li></ul>
<p>Hourly work shows up when the matter goes off script: a substantive refusal, an opposition, or a letter to someone using your mark.</p>
<p>What is excluded matters too. Ask before you sign:</p>
<ul><li>Are office action responses included, or billed separately?</li><li>Is the Statement of Use covered for an intent-to-use filing?</li><li>Are the Section 8 and renewal filings years from now covered?</li><li>Are watch services, demand letters or litigation in scope?</li></ul>
<p>A low flat fee that excludes office action responses is not a low fee. It is half a fee for a job that may need all of one.</p>
<h2 id="a-worked-example-clean-filing-against-one-refusal">A worked example: clean filing against one refusal</h2>
<p>Two applicants, same one-class budget, very different outcomes. The attorney figures below are illustrative, not a quote.</p>
<p><strong>Applicant A, clean filing.</strong> She runs a ceramics studio and sells under an invented word. A search shows nothing close in her class, and her description comes straight from the ID Manual, so no surcharge applies.</p>
<ul><li>USPTO filing fee, one class: $350</li><li>Attorney flat fee for search, drafting and filing: $1,200</li><li><strong>Total to registration: $1,550</strong></li></ul>
<p>Registration issues in roughly a year. The first future item is the Section 8 declaration in year five.</p>
<p><strong>Applicant B, one office action.</strong> He sells clothing. To skip the flat fee he files himself and types his own description into the free-form text box: "clothing and accessories." It is too vague to examine. The USPTO charges the $200 free-form fee, then issues an office action asking him to state exactly what the goods are.</p>
<ul><li>USPTO filing fee, one class: $350</li><li>Free-form text box fee: $200</li><li>Attorney fee to answer the office action: $1,300</li><li><strong>Total: $1,850</strong></li></ul>
<p>He spent $300 more than the applicant who did it properly, arrived two months later, and ended up with a narrower description than he wanted.</p>
<p>Doing it right the first time is not a premium. It is usually the cheaper route.</p>
<h2 id="the-cheap-filing-trap">The cheap-filing trap</h2>
<p>Filing services advertise the $350 figure as if it were the whole cost. Three things go wrong, and each one has a price.</p>
<p><strong>The free-form text box.</strong> The USPTO gives you a menu of pre-approved descriptions. Type your own instead and you pay $200 extra per class. That menu exists because those descriptions have already been judged acceptable. Writing from scratch is not more original. It is more expensive.</p>
<p><strong>The insufficient information fee.</strong> Vague wording, or a missing required detail, adds $100 per class. "Accessories" and "consulting services" are the usual offenders.</p>
<p><strong>The office action.</strong> This is where the real money goes. A refusal has to be answered within the deadline or the application dies, and attorney time on a response commonly runs from several hundred dollars to well over a thousand. Miss the deadline and reviving the application costs $250, and that is not available in every situation.</p>
<p>One test before you file: could a stranger read your description and know exactly what you sell? If not, it is not ready. If you have already been refused, <a href="/blog/what-to-do-if-trademark-application-is-rejected/">what to do if your trademark application is rejected</a> walks through the options.</p>
<h2 id="total-cost-to-registration-and-the-cost-of-keeping-it">Total cost to registration, and the cost of keeping it</h2>
<p>Registration is not the last payment. US trademark law requires you to prove continued use at set intervals, and to renew.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Year five to six:</strong> Section 8 declaration of use, $325 per class. Miss it and the registration is cancelled.</li><li><strong>After five years:</strong> Section 15 declaration of incontestability, $250 per class. Optional, and it makes the mark harder to challenge later.</li><li><strong>Every ten years:</strong> renewal, $650 per class.</li></ul>
<p>For a one-class registration, budget $325 in the fifth year, or $575 if you file the Section 15 declaration at the same time. At each ten-year mark the renewal and its Section 8 declaration are filed together for $650 per class, not as two separate fees. Those fees are small next to what the right is worth, but they arrive long after everyone has stopped thinking about the application.</p>
<h2 id="what-it-costs-not-to-register">What it costs not to register</h2>
<p>The fees are easy to see. The cost of skipping registration is not, which is exactly why people skip it.</p>
<p>Without a federal registration you have no presumption of nationwide exclusive rights. You fall back on common law use, which is tied to the territory where you actually sold. In a dispute, the registered owner generally starts from a stronger position, and you may be the one who rebrands.</p>
<p>Concrete consequences business owners run into:</p>
<ul><li><strong>Marketplaces.</strong> Amazon's Brand Registry and similar programs generally require a registered mark. Without one you lose the tools that remove counterfeit listings.</li><li><strong>Imports.</strong> Registration can be recorded with US Customs to block infringing imports. No registration, no recordation.</li><li><strong>Rebranding.</strong> A new name means signage, packaging, labels, a domain, ads, and the slow loss of whatever recognition you built.</li><li><strong>Enforcement.</strong> A demand letter without a registration is a weaker instrument. The other side can ignore it more easily.</li></ul>
<p>None of that fits in a fee table.</p>
<h2 id="getting-the-number-right-for-your-business">Getting the number right for your business</h2>
<p>The government fee is fixed and published, so that part is knowable today. Everything else depends on your mark, your classes, and how carefully the application is written.</p>
<p>Budget for the whole path to registration rather than the filing alone, and nothing in month eight will come as a shock. If you are deciding whether to file yourself, or you have already been refused, our San Francisco office can review the mark and the description before anything goes on file. A review now costs less than a response later.</p>
<p>If you want the source material first, the USPTO sets out <a href="https://www.uspto.gov/trademarks/basics">how registration works</a>. When you are ready to talk it through, Sulfab Law Office is at (415) 360-5266.</p>
<h2 id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<h3 id="is-350-the-total-cost-of-a-trademark">Is $350 the total cost of a trademark?</h3>
<p>No. That is the government filing fee for one class. Attorney fees, additional classes, description surcharges and maintenance filings come on top. A realistic one-class budget to reach registration commonly falls between about $1,500 and $3,500 before you hit a single refusal.</p>
<h3 id="does-a-name-and-a-logo-cost-twice-as-much">Does a name and a logo cost twice as much?</h3>
<p>Usually, yes. A word mark and a logo are generally filed as two separate applications, so you pay the $350 per class twice plus attorney time for each. Many businesses file the name first and the logo later, once the design has settled and revenue supports the second fee.</p>
<h3 id="why-was-i-charged-200-more-than-the-advertised-fee">Why was I charged $200 more than the advertised fee?</h3>
<p>You likely used the free-form text box instead of the USPTO's pre-approved description list, which adds $200 per class. A vague description can also trigger the $100 insufficient information fee per class. Both are avoidable with careful drafting before you submit.</p>
<h3 id="what-does-it-cost-to-answer-an-office-action">What does it cost to answer an office action?</h3>
<p>Attorney time on a response to a USPTO office action commonly runs from several hundred dollars to well over a thousand, depending on the refusal. The deadline is real. Miss it and the application goes abandoned, and a petition to revive costs $250. <a href="/blog/what-happens-after-receiving-a-uspto-office-action/">What happens after a USPTO office action</a> walks through the process.</p>
<h3 id="what-are-the-ongoing-fees-after-registration">What are the ongoing fees after registration?</h3>
<p>Between year five and six you file a Section 8 declaration of use at $325 per class. Every ten years you renew at $650 per class. The optional Section 15 incontestability declaration is $250 per class after five years of continuous use. Skipping the Section 8 cancels the registration.</p>
<h3 id="is-it-cheaper-to-file-without-an-attorney">Is it cheaper to file without an attorney?</h3>
<p>Up front it looks cheaper. In practice the savings often disappear into a free-form surcharge, an insufficient information fee, or an office action response. Many applicants who file alone end up hiring counsel to fix the application, which means paying twice.</p>
<h3 id="how-long-does-a-trademark-registration-last">How long does a trademark registration last?</h3>
<p>Ten years from the registration date, and you can renew it every ten years for as long as you keep using the mark. Each ten-year filing combines the renewal with a Section 8 declaration of use at $650 per class. Miss the Section 8 in year five, or miss a renewal, and the registration is cancelled or expires, even if the brand is still in use.</p>
